## A chart without a logarithm branch

Write U0=F(a0). Use the16 Hermitian two-qubit Pauli matrices P_mu, including I, with Tr(P_mu P_nu)=4 delta_mu,nu. For a Hermitian X, its real coefficients x_mu=Tr(P_mu X)/4 satisfy ||x||2=||X||F/2<=||X||op. Define the real coordinate map

    G(a)=coeff[(U0*F(a)-F(a)*U0)/(2i)].

Then G(a0)=0 and its real16-by16 Jacobian has columns

    A_mu,j=Tr[P_mu(-i U0* partial_j F(a0))]/4.       (1)

The expression is real because U0* partial_j F is skew-Hermitian. The center direction is included in the minor and scalar derivatives are not discarded.

Each pulse has integrated derivative norm c=2 integral psi=16/15. Complete-unitary Duhamel formulas give ||partial_j F||<=c and ||partial_j partial_k F||<=c^2 for every selected pair, including j=k. For repeated derivatives in one pulse the two time orderings cancel the simplex factor1/2. Unitary factors before/after the pulse do not change these bounds. With m=16 selected parameters, the real-coordinate derivative obeys

    ||DG(a)-DG(b)||2<= L ||a-b||2,  L=m c^2,        (2)

on the box. Indeed the matrix second directional derivative is bounded by c^2||u||1||v||1<=m c^2||u||2||v||2, and taking the Hermitian part and normalized Pauli coordinates cannot increase that bound.

Suppose an actual rational certificate gives sigma_min(A)>=s>0. Choose a positive rational

    t<=min(1/8, s/(2L), 1/(4c sqrt(m))),
    rho=s t/2,   0<r<=min(rho/2,1/8).              (3)

Since sqrt(m)=4, all these constants can be rational. For ||y||2<=rho, the fixed-point map x -> A^{-1}[y-G(a0+x)+Ax] maps the radius-t ball to itself and has contraction at most Lt/s<=1/2: its image norm is at most t/2+Lt^2/(2s)<=3t/4. Thus G covers that real coordinate ball, with parameter displacement strictly inside the allowed box.

Also ||F(a0+x)-U0||<=c sqrt(m)t<=1/4. Any target unitary V with ||V-U0||<r has ||G(V)||2<=r<rho and ||U0*V-I||<1/8. Both U0*F and U0*V then have positive definite real Hermitian part. A unitary with a given imaginary Hermitian part B and positive real Hermitian part is unique: unitarity implies [Re U,Im U]=0 and Re U=sqrt(I-B^2). Therefore equality of G coordinates implies F(a)=V. This proves the full-U(4) endpoint-ball inclusion once s is actually certified. It does not infer a radius from a floating determinant alone.

## A rigorous finite enclosure route

All nominal amplitudes, pulse polynomial coefficients, K and P are rational complex matrices. A candidate selector may use complex128, but the actual certificate should use directed rational or integer interval operations, with original executable source frozen before launch. No empirically chosen ODE tolerance is a theorem premise.

Divide each unit pulse into8 steps of length h=1/8. On a step starting at x0 in[0,7/8], expand psi(x0+z)=sum_(k=0)^4 p_k z^k exactly. Uniform coefficient bounds are |p0|<=1, |p1|<=8, |p2|<=16, |p3|<=32 and |p4|=16. For the local unitary U and its amplitude derivative D use the8-by8 block ODE with generator

    B(z)=[[ -i(K+aP psi), -iP psi ],[0,-i(K+aP psi) ]].

Its fundamental block is [[U,D],[0,U]]. Taylor coefficients C_n obey C_0=I and

    C_(n+1)=(1/(n+1)) sum_(k=0)^min(4,n) B_k C_(n-k).

Equivalently compute coupled4-by4 U,D recurrences. With a<=1, ||B_k|| is bounded by coefficients of

    b(z)=7+32z+64z^2+128z^3+64z^4.

The positive majorant exp(integral_0^z b) bounds the norm of each Taylor coefficient. At R=1/4 its exponent is less than4. Truncation after degree N at h=R/2 therefore has operator-norm error at most

    epsilon_step=81 / 2^(N+1),                    (4)

because exp(4)<3^4=81 and the positive-coefficient tail at R/2 is bounded by2^(-N-1) times the whole sum at R. The full block, including the derivative, has this bound. Select N=80 before the enclosure run. Directed interval arithmetic encloses the exact truncated polynomial; (4) separately pays the analytic tail.

For one selected amplitude, only its pulse has an off-diagonal derivative source. Any exact partial augmented propagator through the entire word has norm at most1+c<3, since its derivative norm is bounded by c and its two diagonal blocks are unitary. With at most n_steps=192 approximate steps, each error at most epsilon_step, exact/approximate telescoping gives

